# sockets and subuids; the installed helper refuses A's volume request carrying B's ids by name. The set's own
# bin/ is both the base and the SPK binaries (spk-host, mini-spk-broker, mini-spk-volume-helper). Root-owned
# files are made only by the journey's installer and removed by the journey's EXIT trap (which runs on failure
# too); when that trap never reported (the journey was killed), the row runs the journey's own `cleanup` mode.
# The journey runs AS the operator: MINI_OPERATOR_UID / MINI_OPERATOR_NAME (default 1001 / ember, the burst
# boxes, where the runner IS the operator and nothing below changes). A runner that is not the operator (hbox: the
# pipeline is uid 1000, the operator mini-operator uid 1001) runs it through a scoped sudoers rule naming only
# two-world-journey.sh (RUNNER ALL=(OPERATOR) NOPASSWD: <tree>/scripts/spk-platform/two-world-journey.sh, env_keep
# MINI_OPERATOR_UID MINI_OPERATOR_NAME), the operator holding root only for that tree's install-user-world. Its run
# root is then the operator's own /run/user/UID/m6g-ID (the runner's RUN is 0700); the row mirrors the evidence it
# reads back into RUN/g with the runner's sudo and removes the operator's root after.
# Inputs a box may lack (the operator account with linger, its sudo rules, sudo -n, uidmap, a subuid range for the
# operator, bwrap, the pinned SPK) are 98.
